<p>Though I didn't like this at first, I'm OK with it now. What this shows is that the D-class is trying to think like a stereotypical robot: logic only, with no thinking outside the box. For example, he could respond by saying that the answer has caused a problem with the tests's logic and it would be necessary to set up a new set of rules. But he can't.</p>
